2026-08-02 (no PR yet -- fill in "PR #N" before merging, per CONTRIBUTING.md) (0 -> 0, no change in row count): First run of the third oracle grid (Tools/OracleGrid/grid-jpl.tsv), the first port-versus-C measurement of the SEFLG_JPLEPH backend at any level. The list stays EMPTY and scripts/verify-oracle.ps1 -Grid Jpl is therefore RED. That is deliberate: 1,985 of 2,400 rows differ, every one of them traced to a single port defect (below), and writing them into a known-diff list would record a defect as an accepted exception instead of fixing it. This entry exists to record the measurement, not to make the gate green.

DATA FILE. NASA JPL DE406, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/ftp/eph/planets/Linux/de406/lnxm3000p3000.406, renamed to de406.eph (a rename only -- JPL's Linux binary IS the .eph format swe_set_jpl_file expects). Size 199,437,056 bytes. SHA-256 b23009e208d625c5e830c4cb67e6313d7f9eadeffe17292a7471f33250c9342d. MD5 39e63b24f3540b92ec83be008f20d70e, which does NOT match the 1ef768440cc1617b6c8ad27a9a788135 Astrodienst publish for their own "de406e.eph"; the two are different files and the SHA-256 above is the authority for reproducing this run. The file's own header record gives ss = [625360.5, 2816848.5, 64] (calendar years -2999 to +3000) and DENUM 406, which is what puts it over the jpldenum >= 403 threshold discussed below. It is not committed and never will be: 190 MB, and the grid is opt-in via -JplFile / SWISSEPH_ORACLE_JPL_FILE precisely so CI never needs it.

WHY THE ORACLE AND NOT THE CONFORMANCE CORPUS. setest hardcodes swe_set_jpl_file("de431.eph") at suite scope, so t.exp's expected values are DE431's and running that corpus against any other DE file yields real differences indistinguishable from port defects (the one-time DE431 run is recorded in Tests/conformance/regenerations.log, 2026-07-31, 500 of 538 JPL rows passing). The oracle has no such constraint: both sides open the same file on the same machine over the same inputs and their raw IEEE-754 bit patterns are compared against each other, so a difference is a port defect by construction.

RESULT. 2,400 rows: 415 bit-identical, 1,985 differing -- 1,860 with a differing return code, 115 differing only in value(s), 10 differing only in the error string. All three groups have the same cause.

ROOT CAUSE: SwissEphNet/CPort/SweJPL.cs rejects any DE file whose constant-name block is not valid single-byte UTF-8. swejpl.c:210 and :682 both read the 400 six-byte constant names as a raw BYTE field -- fread(js->ch_cnam, 1, 6*400, fp) -- and compare the returned BYTE count against 2400. The port reads the same 2400 bytes but decodes them as text (CFile.ReadChars) and compares the resulting CHARACTER count against 2400 (SweJPL.cs:245-247 in fsizer, :743-745 in state). DE406's block contains 176 bytes above 0x7F sitting in the unused slots past its 156th constant, so 2400 bytes decode to 2380 characters under the port's UTF-8 default, the != 2400 guard fires, fsizer returns NOT_AVAILABLE, and open_jpl_file fails. swe_calc then falls back through SEFLG_SWIEPH to Moshier on every row that needs an ephemeris. Measured on both files present here: de406.eph 176 such bytes, 2400 -> 2380 characters; de431.eph zero such bytes, 2400 -> 2400 characters. That is why the DE431 conformance run above never saw this -- the defect is data-dependent and DE431 happens to be clean ASCII there. See docs/known-issues.md, "SweJPL rejects a DE file whose constant-name block is not plain ASCII", for the full write-up. NOT fixed here: SwissEphNet/CPort/ is a frozen path, and although this qualifies as the freeze's one permitted exception (a cited divergence from the C), it is a porting change that belongs in its own reviewed commit with its own manifest update and its own re-measurement, not folded into the commit that built the measurement.

HOW THE THREE GROUPS FOLLOW FROM IT. (1) The 1,860 retc differences are the fallback itself: the C keeps SEFLG_JPLEPH (every C retc is odd), the port returns SEFLG_MOSEPH. (2) The 115 value-only differences are bodies that need no ephemeris file of their own -- mean node (10) and mean apogee (12) -- reached through swe_calc_ut or SEFLG_SIDEREAL: swe_deltat_ex's ephemeris branch (swephlib.c:3318-3353) reads swed.jpldenum, which only an actually-open JPL file sets, and the sidereal frame needs a Sun/Earth position that did fall back, so the UT-to-ET conversion and the ayanamsa differ in their last bits. Their ET, non-sidereal counterparts are bit-identical, which is the check that confirms this reading. (3) The 10 err-only differences are the SEFLG_JPLHOR Earth rows, and they are the most direct evidence of all -- see below.

load_dpsi_deps IS REACHED, ON THE C SIDE, FOR THE FIRST TIME. That function (sweph.c:1380) has exactly one caller in the whole library: swe_set_jpl_file, on the branch where the file it just opened reports jpldenum >= 403 (sweph.c:1503-1504). No other grid, the characterization baseline, or any unit test calls swe_set_jpl_file at all, so before this grid it had no coverage of any kind. plaus_iflag (sweph.c:6121-6141) makes its outcome observable in the err column: 110 of the C dump's SEFLG_JPLHOR rows read "file eop_1962_today.txt not found; default to SEFLG_JPLHOR_APPROX", which is swed.eop_dpsi_loaded == -1 and can only be written by load_dpsi_deps having run. The port's corresponding rows read "you did not call swe_set_jpl_file(); default to SEFLG_JPLHOR_APPROX", which is swed.eop_dpsi_loaded == 0 -- the untouched initial value. So the measurement says precisely this: the C reaches load_dpsi_deps and the port does not, for the same reason everything else here differs. Coverage of the function's own parsing loop is still nil on both sides: neither eop_1962_today.txt nor eop_finals.txt is present, so the C reaches only its swi_fopen-fails early return. Astrodienst distribute both files but they were not retrievable from www.astro.com/ftp/swisseph/ephe/ when this was run (404).

ENVIRONMENT. Windows 11, MSVC x64 (/O2 /fp:precise /MD, the same flags Tools/CReference/build-c.ps1 uses), libswe-2.10.03.lib built from external/swisseph at v2.10.3final, port built from this branch. The 2.08 driver was deliberately not run against this grid -- scripts/classify-oracle-versions.ps1 has no JPL grid wired into it, so a 2.08 JPL dump would be produced and never read.

2026-08-02 (no PR yet -- fill in "PR #N" before merging, per CONTRIBUTING.md) (0 -> 0, no change in row count): Re-measurement after the root cause above was fixed. The list stays EMPTY and scripts/verify-oracle.ps1 -Grid Jpl is now GREEN, with nothing waived. Same grid, same DE406 (sha256 b23009e208d625c5e830c4cb67e6313d7f9eadeffe17292a7471f33250c9342d), same MSVC C reference, same machine: 2,400 of 2,400 rows bit-identical, 0 differing, down from 415 and 1,985. All three groups of the earlier run cleared together, which is what a single shared cause predicts -- 1,860 retc, 115 value-only and 10 err-only. The err-only group is the one worth naming: the port now writes "file eop_1962_today.txt not found; default to SEFLG_JPLHOR_APPROX" on all 110 SEFLG_JPLHOR rows, so it is reaching load_dpsi_deps and taking the same swi_fopen-fails early return the C takes. That function's parsing loop is still uncovered on both sides, for want of the EOP files themselves. The fix is SwissEphNet/CPort/SweJPL.cs reading and counting bytes at both sites instead of decoded characters; frozen path, so it carries freeze-manifest log entry 14 and cites swejpl.c:210-211 and :682-683. The other two grids are untouched either side: dump-net.tsv b36a007e... and dump-net-files.tsv f3fa03aa... are byte-identical before and after.

